The genealogy of Jesus.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah the father of Perez and Zerah, and Tamar their mother. And Perez the father of Hezron. And Hezron the father of Aram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab, and Amminadab was the father of Nahshon, and Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of David the king, and David the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa, the son of Abijah, the son of Jehoshaphat, the son of Asa, the son of Jehoshaphat, the son of Asa, the son of Jehoshaphat, the son of Asa, the son of Abijah, the son of Rehoboam.
Akhaz and his son Isaiah, and Isaiah's son is Uriel, and Uriel's son is Abednego, and Abednego's son is Mishael.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
After this, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. And Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel is the father of Abiud. Abiud is the father of Eliakim. Eliakim is the father of Ezra.
Asaweru walada Zaduqu, wa Zaduqu walada Akimu, wa Akimu walada Aljud.
Alyaud was the father of Eleazar, and Eleazar was the father of Matan, and Matan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. And Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
So the generations of their descendants were, from Adam to Noah, ten generations; from Noah to Abraham, ten generations; from Abraham to David, ten generations; from David to the deportation to Babylon, ten generations; from the deportation to Babylon to the Messiah, ten generations. The sum of the generations is fourteen generations from Abraham to the Messiah, the expected one.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about: His mother Mary was betrothed to Joseph, but before they came together, she was found to be with child from the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a righteous man. He did not want to publicly shame her. So he planned to quietly divorce her.
But as Joseph was thinking about this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, saying, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife, for the Child who has been conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."
She will have a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and she will name him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God is with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not consummate their marriage until she gave birth to her son, and Joseph gave him the name Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea. This happened while Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
So they asked, saying, "Where is the one who has been born king of the Jews? For we saw his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and so were all the people in Jerusalem.
Herod called together all the chief priests and scribes of the people and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem in Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet: 'But you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are not the least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"
"And you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, small among the clans of Judah, from you shall come forth for me one who will be a ruler of Israel, and whose origin is from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5: 2)
Herod secretly called the priests and scribes, and carefully inquired of them the time when the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem, saying, "Go and search carefully for the young child. And when you have found him, report to me, so that I may also go and worship him."
And after the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. And the star that they had seen in the east went before them, until finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. And there they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another way. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
When the Magi had departed, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying, "Arise, take the young child and his mother, and flee into Egypt, and be there until I bring you word; for Herod the king seeks to destroy the young child."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until after the death of King Herod, in order that what the Lord had said through the prophets might be fulfilled: "Out of Egypt I have called my son" (Hosea 11:1).
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding areas. All the boy babies who were two years old and under were to be killed. This corresponded with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
Thus the words of the prophet Jeremiah, which he spoke, were fulfilled. He had said:
A voice is heard in Ramah, it is the voice of weeping and deep sorrow. Rachel weeps for her children, she refuses to be comforted, because they are gone." (Jeremiah 31:15) Jesus' family returns to Nazareth.
After Herod died, if Joseph saw a dream while he was still in Egypt. And if an angel of the Lord appeared to him in the dream.
The angel said, "Rise and take the boy and his mother and go to the land of Israel, for those who were seeking to destroy the boy are dead."
Then Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother by night, and departed to the land of Israel.
But afterwards he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea, and that Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. And Joseph became afraid to go there. And Joseph, after being warned in a dream, returned to the land of Galilee instead.
So he stayed there in a city called Nazareth, to fulfill what was said through the prophets: "Behold, a virgin will be with child and will give birth to a son, and they will call him Emmanuel," which means "God is with us."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Depart from your sins! The kingdom of heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke, saying: "A voice cries out in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight in the desert a highway for our God.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel's hair, and he wore a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to him from Jerusalem and from all Judea and from all the region around the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the River Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to where he was baptizing, and he said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
Produce fruit worthy of repentance for your sins.
Do not suppose that you can enter the kingdom of heaven by saying, "We belong to Abraham." I tell you that God is able to raise up children for Abraham from these stones.
The axe is already lying at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent. But after me will come one more powerful than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
From his sickle in his hand to remove the straw from his school. He gathers his wheat in the barn. As for the chaff, he burns it with the fire that cannot be extinguished." The Baptism of Jesus
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan, and he wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him, saying to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why do you come to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is proper for us to do this. He is executing the holy plan of God." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. And at that moment, the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending upon him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. There, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
The tempter came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command that these stones become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
So Satan took him to the holy city, and stopped him on a pinnacle of the highest temple.
He said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command that these stones become loaves of bread." (Matthew 4:3)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you prostrate yourself to me and worship me," he said, "I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Go away, Satan! For it is written, 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him alone you shall serve.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. And angels came and served him. Jesus begins to preach.
And when Jesus heard that John had been delivered into custody, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the town of Capernaum, which was located on the shore of the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled. He had said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! The land along the Mediterranean Sea! The territory east of the Jordan River!
The people walking in darkness will see a great light. You who live in the land of deep darkness, A light will shine on you." (Isaiah 9:1,2)
From that time, Jesus began to preach, saying, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand." Jesus Chooses the First Disciples
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Going ahead from there, he saw another two brothers, James the son of Zebedee and John his brother, and they were in a boat with their father Zebedee, preparing their nets. And Jesus called them.
Immediately they left the boat, and their father, and followed Jesus. Jesus heals sick people.
Jesus went throughout Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the good news of the kingdom of God, and healing every disease and illness among the people.
His news spread throughout all of Syria, and people were coming to him with everyone who was sick with various diseases. Some were suffering with severe pains, and others were possessed by demons, and some were trembling severely, and others were not at all able to move. And Jesus healed them all.
A great multitude followed him. People came from Galilee, from the region of the ten cities, from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up into the mountain, and sat down. And great multitudes followed him.
Then he began to teach them. And he said:
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the humble, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for the sake of righteousness, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when people insult you and persecute you and say all kinds of false things against you because of me.
Rejoice and be exceedingly glad, for great is your reward in heaven. Thus, also, persecuted they the prophets which were before you. "Salt and Light"
You are the salt of the earth. But what if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it become salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. People will trample it under their feet.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket, but rather puts it on a lampstand, so that those who come in may see the light.
Furthermore, people don't light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then the lamp illuminates everyone in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish but to complete.
What I am about to tell you is true. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not an smallest letter or stroke of the pen will disappear from the Law until everything is accomplished.
Do not disregard even the least of these commandments. And do not teach others to disregard them. For if you do, you will be called the least in the Kingdom of Heaven. Rather, practice these commandments and teach them. And you will be called great in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you: You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and the teachers of the law. If you are not, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. You have heard that it was said to the people long ago, 'You shall not murder; and anyone who murders will be subject to judgment.'
You have heard it said to the people long ago, 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13). Whoever murders will be subject to judgment.
But this is what I tell you: Do not be angry with your brother. Everyone who is angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. And whoever says to his brother: 'You good-for-nothing!' will be subject to the Sanhedrin. And whoever says: 'You fool!' will be liable to fiery Gehenna.
If your offering, when you bring it to the altar, you remember that your brother has something against you,
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
If anyone has a claim against you, go to him and settle the matter between you and him, while you are both on the road, lest he drag you before the judge and the judge hand you over to the tax collector, and you are thrown into prison.
What I am about to tell you is true. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, 'Do not commit adultery' (Exodus 20:14).
Here is what I say to you: Whoever looks at a woman to desire her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to lose one part of your body than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
"If your hand causes you to stumble, cut it off. It is better for you. For you would be bringing all of your body into hell. Divorce."
It was said, "When a man puts away his wife, let him give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I tell you: Whoever divorces his wife, he causes her to commit adultery; and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man cannot divorce his wife unless she has not been faithful to him. Amen.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the covenants you make with the Lord your God. You shall not break your solemn covenants, because you have a right to the promises of the Lord your God."
But that's what I'm telling you: Never make promises like that. Don't make them in the name of heaven. That's God's throne.
Do not make them in the name of the earth; for it is the footstool of God's feet. And do not make them in the name of Jerusalem; for it is the city of the great King.
And do not make your oaths a binding contract on your heads, so you acquire sin and enmity. Is it not just a single hair that you can make white or black?
Let "yes" mean "yes" only. And let "no" mean "no". Anything more than this comes from the evil one. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But I tell you this: Do not resist an evil person. If someone strikes you on your right cheek, turn to him your other cheek also.
If anyone wants to take you to court to get your shirt, give them your cloak as well.
If someone compels you to go one mile, go with him two miles.
Give to anyone who asks of you. Do not turn away from anyone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, 'Love your neighbor and hate your enemy.' (Leviticus 19:18)
But I tell you: Love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you.
So you will be called children of the Father in heaven. For he makes his sun rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even the tax collectors do the same thing.
If you were only greeting your brothers, just that, what else are you doing that others aren't? Even those who don't fear God do that.
Be you, then, perfect even as your Father in heaven is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware of doing "good deeds" before others. Do not do them so that others may see you. If you do, you will not be rewarded by your Father who is in heaven.
When you give to the needy, do not sound a trumpet before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, so that they may be seen by men. I tell you the truth: they have received their reward. When you give alms, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing, so that your alms may be in secret; and your Father who sees in secret will reward you. And when you pray, you must not be like the hypocrites; for they love to stand and pray in the synagogues and at the corners of the streets, so that they may be seen by men. I tell you the truth, they have received their reward. But when you pray, go into your room and shut the door and pray to your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees in secret will reward you. And when you fast, do not look somber as the hypocrites do, for they disfigure their faces so as to show men that they are fasting. I tell you the truth, they have received their reward. But when you fast, anoint your head and wash your face, so that your fasting may be seen not by men, but by your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees in secret will reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your giving should be secret. Your father, who sees in secret, will reward you. Prayer.
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may be seen by others. Truly I say to you, they have received their reward.
When you pray, go into your rooms and close your doors and pray to your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees in secret will reward you. For your Father knows the things you have need of before you ask Him. In this manner, then, pray: Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name. Thy kingdom come. Thy will be done, as in heaven, so on earth. Give us this day our daily bread, and forgive us our debts, as we forgive our debtors. And lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil. For thine is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, for ever and ever. Amen. For where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them. Mat 18:20. Pray without ceasing. 1Th 5:17. A man ought always to pray, and not lose heart. Luk 18:1. Watch and pray, that you enter not into temptation. Mat 26:41. Pray for us, for we also are sure that your work of faith stands first, and over all things for which we give thanks to God always for you all. 2Th 1:11. I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that you present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable to God, which is your reasonable service. And be not conformed to this world, but be you transformed by the renewing of your mind, that you may prove what is that good, and acceptable, and perfect will of God. Rom 12:1-2. I thank God, whom I serve from my forefathers, according to the will of God, the Father, of an incorruptible inheritance, undefiled, and which does not fade away, reserved in heaven for you. 1Pe 1:3-4. Seeing then that we are compassed about with so great a cloud of witnesses, let us lay aside every weight, and the sin which so easily ensnares us, and let us run with endurance the race that is set before us, looking unto Jesus, the author and finisher of our faith, who for the joy that was set before Him endured the cross, despising the shame, and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God. Heb 12:1-2. But speaking the truth in love, may grow up in all things into Christ, who is the head, the body, and you who are His body, and individually members of it. Eph 4:15-16. I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that you present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable to God, which is your reasonable service. And be not conformed to this world, but be you transformed by the renewing of your mind, that you may prove what is that good, and acceptable, and perfect will of God. Rom 12:1-2. I thank God, whom I serve from my forefathers, according to the will of God, the Father, of an incorruptible inheritance, undefiled, and which does not fade away, reserved in heaven for you. 1Pe 1:3-4. Seeing then that we are compassed about with so great a cloud of witnesses, let us lay aside every weight, and the sin which so easily ensnares us, and let us run with endurance the race that is set before us, looking unto Jesus, the author and finisher of our
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may be seen by men. I tell you the truth: they have received their reward.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
Thus you pray: "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from evil when we are tempted. Save us from the evil one.
Forgive others when they wrong you, for your Father in heaven forgives you, even as you forgive others.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting
When you fast, do not look somber as the hypocrites do, who try to show how pious they are. They disfigure their faces to show others that they are fasting. I am telling you the truth. They have received their reward in full.
But when you fast, put oil on your head and wash your face.
Furthermore, no one knows that you are fasting, unless your father who is in secret. Your father who sees in secret will reward you.
Do not accumulate for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead, make your wealth for yourselves in heaven, where no moth destroys it, nor does fire consume it. Where no thief approaches, nor does any stealthy intruder reach it.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eye is clear, your whole body will be bright.
But suppose your eyes are bad. Then your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light that is in you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Don't worry.
For what is more important than food for the body, and what more important than clothing for the body?
Look at the birds of the sky, for they neither sow nor reap nor gather into barns; and yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much better than they?
Can you add even an hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you care about clothing? Look at the field flowers, how they grow. They neither spin nor weave.
But here is what I say to you: even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these flowers.
If God does not prepare the grass that is in the field, will He not prepare you more than that? The grass remains only one day, and then it is thrown into the fire the next day. But your faith is very weak!
Therefore do not worry, saying, 'What shall we eat?' or 'What shall we drink?' or 'What shall we wear?'
People who do not know God are pursuing all these things. But your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God. Do his will, and all these things will be given to you as well.
So don't worry about tomorrow, because tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge, and you will not be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard by which you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure by which you measure others.
You look at the speck of sawdust in your friend's eye, but do not notice the beam in your own eye.
How can you tell your friend, "Let me remove a piece of wood shaving from your eye"? How can you say this when your own eye has a piece of wood in it?
Hypocrite! First, take the beam out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the splinter out of your brother's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs, and do not throw your pearls before the swine, lest they trample them under their feet, and turn and tear you. Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you. For everyone who asks receives, and he who seeks finds, and to him who knocks it will be opened.
Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you.
To him who asks, it shall be given. Whoever seeks finds. The door is opened for the one who knocks.
If your son asks you for bread, will you give him a stone?
Or if he asked for a fish, would he give him a serpent?
Although you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father who is in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what is written in the law and the prophets. The great and small doors.
Go through the narrow gate, for the gate is wide and the road is easy that leads to destruction, and many are those who enter through it.
But the gate is narrow and the way of life is rough. Few people find it. The tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You will know them by their deeds. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thorny thickets?
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
A tree is known by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the person who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord! Is not this the name by which we prophesied, and by which we drove out demons, and by which we performed many miracles?'
Then I will declare to them openly, 'I never knew you.' 'Depart from me, you who practice lawlessness!' you builders of wise and foolish alike.
Therefore, everyone who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
Rain falls. Water rises. Winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears these words of mine and does not act on them will be like a foolish person who built his house on sand.
Rain falls. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. So it falls with a loud crashing sound.
Jesus finished saying all these things, and the crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority, and not as their scribes.
Jesus heals a man who had a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain, and a great crowd followed him.
A man who had a skin disease came, knelt down before Jesus, and said, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. He said, "I am willing to do this," "Be 'clean'!" Immediately the man was healed from his skin disease.
Jesus answered and said to him, "See that you do not tell anyone; but go, show yourself to the priest, and offer for your cleansing the sacrifice that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony for them." The Faith of the Centurion, a Roman
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. The centurion asked Jesus for help.
He said, "O master, your servant is lying down in the house, sick and unable to move, and suffering from severe pain."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Sir, I am not worthy for you to enter my house, but just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, 'Go,' and he goes. I say to that one, 'Come,' and he comes. I say to my servant, 'Do this,' and he does it."
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those who were following him, "I tell you the truth, I have not found in Israel such great faith."
I tell you, many will come from the east and the west, and will take their place at the feast with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the Kingdom of Heaven.
As for those who suppose that they are heirs to the kingdom, they will be thrown outside, into the darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth.
Jesus said to the centurion, "Go, as you believed, so let it be done for you." And his servant was healed at that hour.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law lying in bed, suffering from a fever.
Jesus took her by the hand; the fever left her, and she rose and began to serve him.
And when evening came, they brought to Him many who were possessed by demons; and He cast out the spirits by a word, and healed all who were sick.
He did this to confirm what the prophet Isaiah had said: "He took on our illnesses and our suffering; yet we thought he was afflicted, struck down by God, and rejected" (Isaiah 53:4). Following Jesus demands sacrifice.
Jesus saw the crowds around him, so he ordered his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then one of the scribes came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have holes and the birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
And another disciple said to him, "Lord, permit me first to go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm.
Jesus entered a boat, and his disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a terrible storm blew over the lake. The waves were rising over the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples got up and woke him, saying, "Teacher, save us! We're perishing!"
He answered them, "Why are you so afraid? Do you still have no faith?" And Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they subsided, and there was a great calm.
The disciples were amazed and said, "What is this? Even the winds and the sea obey him!" Jesus heals two men who are demons
Jesus arrived on the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. Two men who were possessed by demons met him, for they were coming from the tombs. The men were so violent that no one could pass by that way.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you come here to punish us before the appointed time for judgment against us?"
In the not too distant distance, there was a large herd of pigs grazing.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you are going to drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The herd of pigs rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
Those who tended the pigs fled and went to the city. They reported everything and what had happened to the men who were possessed by demons.
So the whole city of Jerusalem went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to leave their territory.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus got into a boat. He went to the other side of the lake, and came to his own town.
Some men came carrying a man who was paralyzed and lying on a mat. When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, "Cheer up, son! Your sins are forgiven."
Some of the scribes among themselves said in their hearts, "This man blasphemes!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Stand up and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then he said to the paralytic, 'Rise, take up your bed and go to your home.'
The man got up and went home.
When the crowd saw it, they were astonished, and they glorified God who had given such authority to men. (Matthew 9:8)
As Jesus was walking, he saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax office. And he said to him, "Follow me." And Matthew rose up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and sinners came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this and asked his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this and said, "It is not those who are well who need a physician, but those who are ill."
"Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice.' For I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance and to follow me." Jesus was asked about fasting.
The disciples of John came to Jesus and said to him, "We fast often and we offer prayer. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the wedding guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? The days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them; then they will fast in those days.
People do not sew a patch of new cloth on old clothes. The new piece will pull away from the old one. This will make the tear worse.
People don't put new wine into old wine skins. If they do, the skins burst. The wine spills out and the skins are ruined. No, people put new wine into new wine skins. Both are preserved. A dead girl and a suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying these things, one of the rulers of the synagogue came, knelt down before him, and said, "My daughter has just died, but come and lay your hand on her, and she will live."
Jesus stood up and went with him, and the disciples also went.
In her state, that is, when she had just arrived, a woman came forward from behind him, holding the end of his robe. She had been bleeding internally for twelve years, and she had not been able to be healed by anyone.
"I only need to touch his clothes," she thought. "Then I will have my revenge."
Jesus turned and saw her. "Woman, do not lose heart," he said. "I tell you, your faith has healed you." And in that very moment, the woman was healed.
When Jesus entered the leader's house, he saw there flute players and a noisy crowd.
He said, "Go, the girl is not dead, she is sleeping." But they mocked him.
And after the multitude was put outside, Jesus went in and took her by the hand, and the maid arose.
The news about what Jesus had done spread all over that region. Jesus heals two blind men.
While Jesus was going on from there, two blind men followed him, crying out and saying, "Son of David, have mercy on us!"
When Jesus entered the house, the blind men came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" And they answered him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he wiped their eyes, saying, "Why do they say, 'We see clearly'?"
Now they were able to see again. And Jesus warned them strictly saying, "Be sure no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it in all that region.
And as they were going out, a man who was mute was brought to him, by whom a demon was living.
When the evil spirit had left him, the man spoke. And the crowds were astonished, saying, "Such a thing has never been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are few hired hands.
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. He was teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom. And he was healing all manner of sickness and disease.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were exhausted and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few."
"Therefore, you should ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest."
Jesus sent out the twelve disciples.
Jesus summoned his twelve disciples to join him. He gave them authority to drive out demons and heal every disease and affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then comes James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
The following are Philip and Bartholomew, and Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. Two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Judas Thaddeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve with the following orders: "Do not go to the Gentiles, or to the Samaritans in any city of the Samaritans.
Instead of that, go to the people of Israel. They are like lost sheep.
As you go, proclaim, saying: 'The kingdom of the heavens has drawn near.'
Heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons. Freely you have received, freely give.
Do not carry in your bags gold or silver or bronze.
Do not take a travel bag. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker must be given what they need.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at this person's home until you leave.
When you enter the house, greet those who live there.
If that house welcomes you, give it your blessing with peace. And if it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. The day of judgment will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I am sending you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
You shall be brought before governors and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for them and for the Gentiles.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to utter.
It will not be you who speaks, but the Spirit of your Father who will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to death. Fathers will deliver their sons. Children will rise against their parents and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But everyone who stands firm to the end will be saved.
When they attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is true. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is no better than his teacher. The servant is no better than his master.
It is enough for a disciple to be like his master, and it is enough for a servant to be like his master. If the master of the house is called Beelzebul, what will those who live in his house expect?
So fear them not, but fear Me! I have prepared for those who disbelieve a severe punishment. And to Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and to Him is the final return. Do you not see that to Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth? And every nation has a term; when they reach their term, neither can they delay it nor can they advance it. Say: 'Consider what is in the heavens and the earth,' but it will not call to mind to you the unjust; they are only in a straying. Do they not consider that We may destroy what is around them or that We may seize them from themselves? Then would they be able to avert the punishment or to deliver themselves? And to those who were before them, the worst of crimes has been proposed, so they have accepted it. And I had turned them over to the ungodly as a punishment because they took false gods and because they were defiant. And when they had been gravely rebuked, they would not desist, but rather they would act with insolence in the land. And when they had been chosen for gracious treatment, they would say, 'This is for us; and, further, we will be given in addition to this.' But, no indeed! it is they who are ungrateful and unknowing. Say, 'If what you are calling upon, instead of Allah, does not control the provision for you, nor have strength for you; so invoke them, and let them answer you, if you are truthful.'
What I tell you in darkness, proclaim in the light. And do not hide your light under a basket, but place it on a stand.
Do not be afraid of those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. Rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing? Yet not one of them falls to the ground apart from your Father.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
So fear them not, but fear Me, if you are (true) believers.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make "sons to hate their fathers, and daughters to rise up against their mothers, and daughters-in-law to contend against their mothers-in-law.
The man's enemies are those of his household. (Micah 7:6)
Everyone who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. Everyone who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives the one who sent me.
Assume that someone welcomes a prophet as a prophet. That person will receive the reward of a prophet. And assume that someone welcomes a sincere friend as a sincere friend. That person will receive the reward of a sincere friend.
If anyone offers this little one who is following me a cup of cold water just because of me, he will certainly not lose his reward.
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus finished his teaching to his twelve disciples. Then he moved to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who is to come, or shall we look for another?"
Jesus answered, "Go and tell John what you hear and what you see.
The blind see. The disabled walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are brought to life. And good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not abandon their faith for me.
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to address the crowds about John, saying, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
So, what did you go out to see? Someone dressed in fine clothing? No. Those who dress in fine clothing are found in the kings' palaces.
What then did you go out into the wilderness to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one about whom it was written in the Bible: "Behold, I will send My messenger, and he will prepare the way before You." (Malachi 3: 1)
What I am about to tell you is the truth. No one was born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
For John the Baptist's days, the Kingdom of Heavens has been progressing forcefully. And powerful people seize it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John came.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear.
"What are people like today? They are like children sitting in the marketplace calling out to others. They say,
We played the nay for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song for you. But you were not sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink, and people said, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man comes, he will eat and drink as you do. And they will say, 'Here is someone who feasts with sinners.' But wisdom is vindicated by her deeds.' 'The cities that do not repent will be destroyed.'
Jesus began to deny the cities in which he had performed his most miracles. Their inhabitants did not repent of their sins. And he said:
"Woe to you, O Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, which were performed in you, they would have repented long ago, sitting in sackcloth and ashes."
But I tell you: Tyre and Sidon will be more tolerable for them on the day of judgment than you will be.
And as for you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No, you will be brought down to hell. For if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained to this day.
But I tell you: it will be more tolerable for Sodom on the day of judgment than for you." "Rest for all who are weary"
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and prudent and have revealed them to infants."
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
My father has paid for me everything. And the Father is the only one who knows the Son. And the knowledge of the Father is only for the Son and for whom the Son wills to reveal him to them.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Become my disciples and learn from me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus was walking through the wheat fields. His disciples were hungry, so they began to pick the heads of wheat and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this; and they said to Jesus, "Look! This doing of good on the Sabbath is not lawful according to the tradition. But your disciples are doing it on any case!"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David and those with him did?
He entered the house of God and ate bread of the household, the consecrated bread, which it was not lawful for him to eat, except for the priests, and he also gave some of it to his men.
Have you not read the law? It is written how the priests in the temple should do their work on every sabbath, but they are not considered guilty.
I tell you, here is the one greater than the temple.
The inscription says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you did, you would not make accusations against the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
Leaving from that place, Jesus entered their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered, useless hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Does the Law allow us to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "If one of you had a sheep that fell into a pit on the Sabbath, would you not hold it and lift it out?"
Man is more precious than a sheep! So the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath."
Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it was restored, sound and healthy like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and plotted how they might kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew all about the Pharisees' plans. So he left that place. Many followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to tell anyone who he was.
This is to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet Isaiah, saying:
Here is my servant, whom I uphold, my chosen, in whom I delight. I have put my Spirit upon him; he will proclaim justice to the nations. He will not grow faint or be crushed until he establishes justice on earth.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a dim flame. He will make everything alright.
The nations trust in him.
A man who was possessed by demons and was blind and unable to speak was brought to Jesus. Jesus healed him, and the man was able to speak and see.
Everyone was amazed and said, "Is this the son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard that, they said, "This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons."
Jesus knew their thoughts, so he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation; and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is divided against himself. How then can his kingdom stand?
You say, 'I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul.' Then by what power do those who are with you cast them out? Therefore they will be your judges.
But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or think about this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and simply take what he owns? You must first restrain him. Then, you can rob his house.
Whoever is not with me is against me. And whoever does not gather with me the sheep scatters them.
Here is what I say to you: Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Every one who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If the tree is good, its fruit will be good; and if the tree is bad, its fruit will be bad. For a tree is known by its fruit.
O den of vipers! How can you, who are evil, say anything good? Out of the abundance of the heart, the mouth speaks.
A good person says good things, and these things come from the goodness that is within him. And an evil person says evil things, and these things come from the evil that is within him.
But this is what I say to you: On the day of judgment, people will have to give an account for every careless word they have spoken.
By your words you will be judged or acquitted. "The miraculous verse of Jonah"
Then Pharisees and scribes came to Jesus from Jerusalem and said to him, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
He replied, "Those who ask you for a sign are the people of disbelief and corruption. And they will not be given anything like the miracle of the Prophet of God, Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a large fish for three days and three nights. Something like that will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will stand up on the day of judgment with the peoples who are living now. And the Ninevites will prove that those people are guilty. The men of Nineveh repented of their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now there is someone here who is more important than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will rise up on the day of judgment with the peoples who are living now. And she will prove that they are guilty. She came from a very great distance to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now there is One here who is greater than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a man? It goes through dry areas, looking for a place to rest. But it doesn't find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been swept, cleaned, and put in order.
Then the unclean spirit goes out and returns to its house, and the house is swept and put in order. But the unclean spirit returns, and it is seven times more powerful than before.
While Jesus was speaking to the crowds, His mother and His brothers stood outside, seeking to speak to Him.
Someone told him: "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to talk to you."
Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples and said, "Behold, my mother and my brothers!
Everyone who does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On that same day, Jesus left the house and sat down by the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. He entered a boat. He sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things, employing in this parable. He said, "A sower went out to sow his seed.
Scattering the seeds on the ground. Some fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns; the thorns grew up and choked the plants.
Other seeds fell on good soil, and they produced a crop, some a hundredfold, some sixtyfold, and some thirtyfold.
Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear.
His disciples came to him and asked, "Why do you speak to the people in parables?"
He answered them, "Knowledge of the secrets of the kingdom of the heavens has been given to you, but they have not been given."
Those who possess this type of knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have far too much. If no one possesses this type of knowledge, then even the little that someone has will be taken away from them.
This is why I use stories when I talk to people. I say, "They are looking, but they do not really see. They are listening, but they do not really hear or understand."
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled, which he said: "You will hear and not understand, and see and not perceive."
The hearts of these people have become callous, and their ears are hearing heavily, and their eyes they have closed them, lest they see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and turn back and I will heal them. (Isaiah 6: 9, 10)
But blessed are your eyes, for they see; and your ears, for they hear.
What I am about to tell you is a reality. Many prophets and godly people have wanted to see what you see. But they have not seen it. They have wanted to hear what you hear. But they have not heard it.
Listen up! This is the meaning of the farmer's story.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. These people are like the seed that was planted on the road.
And those who have fallen in rocky places are those who hear the word, and immediately they receive it with joy.
But they have no roots, so they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from the faith when trouble or suffering comes because of the message.
And others received the seeds that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the message. But afterward, they are crowded out by the anxieties of daily life and the false promises of wealth. They prevent them from producing the fruit.
But others received the seeds that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a harvest that is a hundred, sixty, or thirty times more than what the farmer planted." "The Weeds Story"
Jesus told another parable to the crowds, saying, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven like. A man sowed good seed in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat started to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The farm owner's servants came to him and said, "Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where then have these weeds come from?"
He answered, "An enemy has done this." And the servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner answered. "While you are pulling out the weeds, you might pull out the wheat along with it."
Let them grow together until the harvest. And in the time of harvest I will say to the reapers, 'Gather the firstfruits of the harvest into my barn.' "As the new testament and the bruised reed are compared.
Jesus told another parable to the crowds, saying, "The kingdom of the heavens is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest plant in the whole garden. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest on its branches.
Jesus told them another parable. "The kingdom of heaven is like leaven," he said. "A woman took yeast and mixed it with three measures of flour, until all the dough had risen."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not speak to them without a parable.
The words of the prophet which he spoke, saying, "I will open my mouth in parables, I will utter things hidden from the foundation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explains the parable of the noxious weeds.
Then Jesus left the crowd and went into the house. His disciples approached him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the tares that is in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are uprooted and burned in the fire. So it will be on the day of judgment.
The Son of Man sends out his angels, and they gather out of his kingdom all causes that offend, and them which do iniquity.
They shall cast them into the furnace of fire: there shall they be devoured. There shall men weep, and they shall lament for the wicked one. They shall gnash their teeth, and they shall clothe themselves with sackcloth: and they shall beat their breasts for the transgressor.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Let the hearer hear. The parables of the hidden treasure and the pearl
The kingdom of the heavens is like a treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and hid; and from joy over it he goes and sells all that he has and buys that field.
The kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.
He found a very precious one. So he went and sold everything he owned. And he bought that pearl. The Parable of the Net.
The kingdom of heaven is like a net cast into the lake, and gathering together every kind of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But the bad fish they threw away.
This is how the Day of Judgment will be. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
They will be thrown into the fire pit with the evil ones. There, the evil ones weep and gnaw their teeth.
Jesus said, "Do you understand all these things?" They answered, "Yes."
He said to them: "Every scribe who has become a disciple of the Kingdom of the Heavens knows that the Kingdom of the Heavens is like a man, a householder, who brought out of his treasure what was new and what was old."
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began teaching people in their synagogues, and they were amazed and said, "Where did this man get this wisdom, and these miraculous powers?"
Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, and his brothers are James, Joses, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not satisfied with him at all. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own town, and in his own house."
He did not make many signs there, except for a few, because they did not have faith in him.
John the Baptist was beheaded.
At that time, reports about Jesus reached Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea.
He said to his servant, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias, the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was saying to Herod, "You have no right to take her; it is unlawful."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they considered John to be a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced before Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
So he promised her that he would give her everything she asked for.
Her mother told her what to say. So the girl said to Herod, "I want the head of John the Baptist on a big plate."
The king was very angry. But he thought of his promise and of his dinner guests. So he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
His head was brought on a large plate and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
And when it was almost evening, his disciples came to him and said, "The place is deserted, and it is already late. Send the crowds away to go to the villages and towns in the surrounding area to buy food for themselves."
Jesus answered, "It is not necessary for them to go. You give them something to eat."
They answered, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
He said, "Bring them here to me."
Then Jesus instructed the crowds to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves of bread and the two fish, and looking up to heaven, He thanked God. Then He broke the loaves of bread and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowds.
Everyone ate and was satisfied, and the disciples gathered twelve baskets full of leftover fragments.
There were about five thousand men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
Jesus compelled his disciples to embark on a boat and precede him to the other side, to the region of Bethsaida, and then he dismissed the crowd.
And after he sent them, he went off alone to the mountain to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was already far from the shore. The waves were hitting it because the wind was blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "It's a ghost!" they cried. And they screamed in fear.
Jesus immediately answered them, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid."
Peter said, "Lord, if it is you, tell me." He said, "I am." Peter said to him, "Lord, if it is you, command me to come to you on the water."
"Come," he said. So Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid and began to sink; and he cried out, saying, "Lord, save me!"
Jesus stretched out his hand, grasped him, and said to him, "O you of little faith, why did you doubt?"
When they got into the boat, the wind died down.
And those who were in the boat worshiped Jesus and said, "Truly you are the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and came to the region of the Gennesaret.
The men who were living there knew Jesus. So they sent a message to all the surrounding regions. And the people brought all their sick to Jesus.
They begged him to allow the sick to simply touch the hem of his robe. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "impure"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked,
Why do your disciples not live according to the teachings of the elders? Your disciples do not wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered: "And why do you not obey the commandment of God? You would rather follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16). And He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death" (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9).
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
Therefore, they have no need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God of no effect for the sake of following your own traditions.
"O ye protestors! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:"
These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only human precepts." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him and said, "Hear and understand."
What goes into your mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of your mouth, that is what defiles you.
Then his disciples came to him and said to him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were disturbed by what they said?"
Jesus answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man. The field is the world, and the good seed represents the righteous, while the weeds represent the wicked. The enemy who sowed the weeds is the devil. The harvest is the end of the age, and the harvesters are angels. Therefore, just as weeds are gathered and burned, so will it be at the end of the age. The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather from his kingdom everything that causes sin and those who practice evil. And they will throw them into the fiery furnace, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears, let them hear."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit."
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Do you still not understand?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth ends up in the stomach. Then it leaves the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false witness, and telling lies about others.
Those are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord, Son of David! Have mercy on me! My daughter is demon-possessed and suffers greatly."
Jesus did not utter a word. His disciples came to him and begged him, saying, "Send her away, for she cries out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was not sent except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees in front of him. "Master! Help me!" she said.
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
She said, "Yes, Lord. But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' tables."
Jesus answered and said to her, "O woman, great is your faith! Let it be to you as you desire." And her daughter was healed from that hour.
Jesus departed from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to the mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought with him blind and lame people. They also brought with him those who were maimed, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
And the people marveled. And they who were not able to speak were speaking. And the afflicted were healed. And the mute were speaking. And the lame were walking. And the blind were seeing. And the people glorified the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said to them, "I have compassion for these people. They have already been with me three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, for if I do, they will become faint on the way home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here at all," they said, "And where can we find enough bread to satisfy these vast multitudes?"
Jesus asked, "How many loaves do you have?" They replied, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus ordered the crowds to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves and the fish, He looked up to heaven and blessed the food. Then, breaking the loaves, He gave them to the disciples to distribute. And they went out into the roads and collected all the sick they could find and brought them to the place where Jesus was. And they healed many who were sick with various diseases and cast out many demons. And in the evening, the disciples came to Jesus and said to Him, "This is a deserted place, and it is now late. Send the crowds away so they can go to the villages and buy themselves something to eat." But Jesus answered them, "You give them something to eat." They said to Him, "Should we go and buy bread worth two hundred denarii and give them something to eat?" And Jesus said to them, "How many loaves do you have? Go and see." And when they found out, they said, "Five loaves and two fish." And Jesus directed the crowds to sit down on the ground. And taking the five loaves and the two fish, He looked up to heaven and blessed the food. Then, breaking the loaves, He gave them to the disciples to distribute. And they went out into the roads and collected all the sick they could find and brought them to the place where Jesus was. And there were so many people that they had nothing to eat. And when Jesus heard it, He said to His disciples, "This is a time of great mercy, but the people do not recognize it. They are like a child who refuses to eat when offered good food." And Jesus went up into the hills and sat down there. And when it was time for the Passover, Jesus sent Peter and John to prepare for the meal. And they said to Him, "Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?" And Jesus said to them, "When you have entered the city, a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him into the house where he lives. And you will find there a large upper room, already furnished. Prepare the Passover for us there." And Peter and John went out and went to the house where the disciples were staying. And they said to Peter and John, "Are you the ones who were told to go and prepare for the meal?" And they said to them, "Go into the city, and a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him into the house where he lives. And you will find there a large upper room, already furnished. Prepare the Passover for us there." And Peter and John went out and went to the house where the disciples were staying. And they said to Peter and John, "Are you the ones who were told to go and prepare for the meal?" And they said to them, "Go into the city, and a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him into the house where he lives. And you will find there a large upper room, already furnished. Prepare the Passover for us there." And Peter and John went out and went to the house where the disciples were staying. And they said to Peter and John, "Are you the ones who were told to go and prepare for the meal?" And they said to them, "Go into the city, and a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him into the house where he lives. And you will find there a large upper room, already furnished. Prepare the Passover for us there." And Peter and John went out and went to the house where the disciples were staying. And they said to Peter and John, "Are you the ones who were told to go and prepare for the meal?" And they said to them, "Go into the city, and a man carrying a jar of water will meet
So all ate and were filled, and the disciples picked up seven basketfuls of leftover fragments.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
And after Jesus had sent away the crowds, he went up into the boat. And he came to the regions of Magdala.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He answered, saying: "In the evening you look at the sky. And you say: 'The weather will be fine. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, "Today there will be a storm. The sky is red and cloudy." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"A generation of vipers, a generation seeking a sign, and no sign will be given to it, except the sign of Jonah." And he left them and went away. "The doctrine of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The disciples crossed over to the other side of the sea. And they forgot to take bread.
Jesus said to them, "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils discussed this amongst themselves, saying, "He must be saying this because we haven't taken any bread."
Jesus knew what they were discussing, so he said to them, "Why are you discussing among yourselves that you have no bread? Do you not yet understand? Are your hearts hardened?"
Do you not yet understand? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets you filled with broken pieces?
Have you not remembered the seven loaves of bread which satisfied the four thousand men? Or how many baskets full of the fragments did you pick up?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus did not refer to the leaven of bread, but to the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Messiah.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. And there he asked his disciples, saying, "Who do people say that I am?"
They answered, "Some say he is John the Baptist; others say he is Elijah; and still others say he is Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
But he asked, "And you? Who do you say that I am?"
Simon Peter answered him, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood have not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
This is what I say to you: You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of Hades will not overcome it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. And whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven. And whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven."
Jesus instructed his disciples not to tell anyone that he was Jesus the Christ. Jesus speaks of his approaching death.
From that time Jesus began to tell his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and be killed, and on the third day rise.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "God forbid it, Lord! This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me; for you are not setting your mind on the things of God, but on the things of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "If anyone wants to follow after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me."
If anyone wants to save his life, he will lose it; but if he loses it for my sake, he will find it.
What good would it do to gain the whole world and lose your own soul? Or what can a man give in exchange for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and will repay each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some of those standing here will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance was changed.
And after six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, the brother of James, and went up with them to a high mountain, by themselves.
There before them, their appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. And his clothes became white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, 'Master, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three shelters: one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.'
And as Peter was still speaking, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them; and a voice out of the cloud said, "This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to Him."
When the disciples heard this, they were filled with awe and fell face down to the ground.
But Jesus approached and touched them, saying, "Arise, and do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And while they were coming down, Jesus told them what they must do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he told them. "Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make everything new again."
But I tell you: Elijah has come already, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they wanted. So also will they do to the Son of Man.
At that time, the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus healing a boy possessed by a demon.
And when they drew near to the crowd, a man approached him and knelt before him on his knees.
"Master," he said, "have mercy on my son. He trembles in a savage way and suffers greatly. He falls often into the fire or into the water."
"I brought him to your disciples, and they could not heal him."
"O generation, non-believing and crooked, until when shall I be with you? Until when shall I bear you?" Jesus answered. "Come to Me with the boy here."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. And the boy was healed in that very hour.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, "Why could we not cast it out?"
He answered them, "Because your faith is so small. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
I
They met together in Galilee. And Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." So the disciples were very sad.
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter and asked him, "Doesn't your teacher pay the temple tax?"
He answered, "Yes, he does." When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "On whom do the kings of the earth take tolls and taxes? On their own sons or on strangers?"
Peter answered: "From others". And Jesus said to him: "Then the sons do not need to pay".
But we don't want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find there the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tax for me and for you.
